   "line": [
    "e5"
   ],
   "spoken": "White pushes the pawn to e5 — but this advance is premature. Unlike the French, where the same structure traps Black's light-squared bishop, here that bishop has not yet been shut in and can escape.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": "The advance is only strong if it locks in the light-squared bishop — get the bishop out first and it loses its point.",
   "plans": "Free the bishop before closing the position with e6."
  },
  {
   "ply": 6,
   "t": 65,
   "fen": "rn1qkbnr/ppp2ppp/4p3/3pPb2/3P4/8/PPP2PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 4",
   "line": [
    "Bf5",
    "d4",
    "e6"
   ],
   "spoken": "The whole idea: develop the light-squared bishop to f5, OUTSIDE the pawn chain, before playing e6. The very piece that sits behind bars in the French is here a free, active bishop.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": "In French-type structures, the problem child is the light-squared bishop — solve it first and the position is pleasant.",
   "plans": "Then build the centre with e6 and c5."
  },
  {
   "ply": 7,
   "t": 73,
   "fen": "rn1qkbnr/ppp2ppp/4p3/3pPb2/3P4/P7/1PP2PPP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq - 0 4",
   "line": [
    "a3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 8,
   "t": 89,
   "fen": "rn1qkbnr/pp3ppp/4p3/2ppPb2/3P4/P7/1PP2PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 5",
   "line": [
    "c5"
   ],
   "spoken": "And now the pawn strikes to c5 in a single move — no need for the Caro-Kann's slow c6-then-c5. The bishop is already free and the central break comes at once: the best of both worlds.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": "Saving a tempo on the freeing break (c5 in one move) is a concrete gain over the Caro move order.",
   "plans": "Pressure the d4-pawn and the centre."
  },
  {
   "ply": 9,
   "t": 94.5,
   "fen": "rn1qkbnr/pp3ppp/4p3/2ppPb2/3P4/P1P5/1P3PPP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq - 0 5",
   "line": [
    "c3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
